  "roles": "3.4.69 gives karman as what lakāra expresses in the stated transitive readings and bhāva in the stated intransitive readings. 1.3.13 prescribes the A endings for both. A patient can be person 1, 2 or 3 in any number. An impersonal activity supplies no plural or personal patient; its finite expression is third singular, even with a non-singular or first-person agent in the instrumental. This selection uses the archived valency of the particular lexical sense.",
  "qualification": "The Bālamanoramā on 3.4.69 includes an ordinarily transitive verb with an unexpressed and unintended object in the intransitive reading: devadattena bhujyate. An omitted word alone need not imply that the object is unintended. No such additional read of the twelve lexical inputs is counted here.",
  "formal_decisions": [
    "In the four sārvadhātuka environments yak before an A ending gives the written y-factor. Its status as an aniṭ ārdhadhātuka suffix is what triggers loss of preceding ṇic in the three curādi entries. The residual radical quantity still distinguishes nāṭyate and śāmyate from the corresponding short-vowel roots.",
    "For the two styai roots yak is kit, not śit. 6.1.45 supplies ā from ai. The named group in 6.4.66 does not include these two roots. Their bhāve third-singular present is styāyate.",
    "Mah of honour and lexical curādi maha yield the same mahyate with different earlier histories. Divādi and class-one sam both yield impersonal samyate; their agentive present stems in PG206 are different."
  ],
  "source_policy": "The published comparison file has a conventional nine-slot middle table for each root. Its eight non-third-singular columns on each intransitive bhāve request are outside the selected syntactic domain. Reused PG221 rod positions are shown without new credit."
}
